[[:db/retract 17592186355854 :question/answer-fields 17592186355855] {:db/id "q-161481/literal-zero-area-v1/selection/answer-zero" :answer/id #uuid "7cd88d8b-1154-5924-84b2-a8255f4e1264" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161481/literal-zero-area-v1/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b0d1580-cb70-50e0-aeb7-fd5dcae8bb21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices [17592186355856 17592186355857 17592186355858 17592186355859 17592186355860 "q-161481/literal-zero-area-v1/selection/answer-zero"] :answer-field/correct "q-161481/literal-zero-area-v1/selection/answer-zero" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186355854 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$\n\nInterpret the printed inequalities literally. If they define a degenerate set, use the zero-area surface-integral convention. What is the flux?\n\nHint: First check whether $-x\\le y\\le0$ can have a nonempty interval when $x\\le0$. Treat the printed inequalities literally." :question/worked-solution "The original inequalities are retained literally. Since $-\\pi\\le x\\le0$, we have $-x\\ge0$. The additional inequalities $-x\\le y\\le0$ are possible only when $-x=0$, so $x=0$ and $y=0$. Therefore the projection is $D=\\{(0,0)\\}$ and its two-dimensional area is zero. The graph $z=\\cos(x+y)$ over $D$ consists only of $(0,0,1)$; it is a degenerate surface, not a regular two-dimensional patch.\n\nUnder the zero-area surface-integral convention, both its surface area and its flux are zero:\n\n$$\\operatorname{area}(S)=0,\\qquad\\iint_S\\langle1,0,y\\rangle\\cdot d\\mathbf S=0.$$\n\nOne may extend the upward graph vector area element from the surrounding smooth graph: $(-g_x,-g_y,1)=(\\sin(x+y),\\sin(x+y),1)$. Its dot product with $F=\\langle1,0,y\\rangle$ is $\\sin(x+y)+y$. Integrating this bounded continuous function over the literal zero-area set $D$ yields zero.\n\nThe source accepted choice b, $\\pi-\\pi^3/6$, and its revealed solution instead evaluated the formal signed repeated integral\n\n$$\\int_{-\\pi}^{0}\\int_{-x}^{0}(\\sin(x+y)+y)\\,dy\\,dx=\\int_{-\\pi}^{0}\\left(1-\\cos x-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right)\\,dx=\\pi-\\frac{\\pi^3}{6}.$$\n\nFor $x<0$, these inner limits are reversed. A formal signed integral over reversed limits does not integrate over the region described by the original inequalities. No corrected bounds are inferred. The study answer is $0$ for the original literal inequalities; the source's choice, grading and calculation remain preserved as evidence." :question/answer-fields ["q-161481/literal-zero-area-v1/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
